WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.200
Rack-average power hides time behavior.

2
00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:05.960
A workload can have the same average demand as another while producing

3
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.380
different excursions, repetition and correlation across equipment.

4
00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:13.300
Those differences can matter at the rack and at the facility interface.

5
00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:19.040
The twenty twenty-six power-oscillation discussion connects changing training demand to supply-side concerns.

6
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:23.360
Use that context to ask what events the proposed system must support.

7
00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:28.280
Identify the magnitude, duration and repetition of the relevant demand changes, and

8
00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:29.960
the boundary where behavior is measured.

9
00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:34.200
Don't infer a hardware response time or permitted excursion from a generic

10
00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:34.600
curve.

11
00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:38.720
Obtain the supported envelope and test evidence for the actual configuration.

12
00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:43.600
Also assign responsibility: which response is local to the equipment or rack,

13
00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:45.160
and which depends on the facility?

14
00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:49.920
A dashboard average can be useful operational information while remaining insufficient for

15
00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:51.040
dynamic qualification.

16
00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:55.220
This is why a steady-state power budget and a time-domain review complement

17
00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:56.700
each other rather than compete.

